  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Consider the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.$ On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to this function?\n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[1,2]$\n- $[-1,0)$",
  "worked_solution": "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\u22481.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
